// Between Frontiers: unit types of 1925–2025 (phase 5, not wired into the simulation yet).
// Contract: crates/bf-sim/src/war/catalogue.rs (UnitsFile); reasoning in docs/war.pt-BR.md.
// Licence: CC BY-SA 4.0. Nothing here comes from a game or a mod: types are roles, not names of
// any game's units.
//
// Element: land 1,000 men; sea one ship; air one aircraft; strategic one weapon, launcher or
// satellite. Equipment and upkeep are per element, in each good's unit (lots of US$ 1,000 at base
// price for the military and capital goods, tonnes for food and fuel).
//
// Every quantity is a DESIGN PARAMETER, not a historical figure: an order of magnitude to be
// calibrated (docs/war.pt-BR.md, "Calibração prevista"). The rules that set them:
// - Food: 5 t of processed_food a week per 1,000 men (0.7 kg a man a day); fodder 30 t of grain
//   for a mounted element (one horse a man, 4 kg a day).
// - Ships: equipment = tonnage × cost per tonne of the class (battleship and cruiser ~US$ 1,000/t,
//   destroyer and escort ~1,250/t, submarine ~1,700/t, later generations more); fuel 1 % of the
//   tonnage a week in peace.
// - Aircraft: equipment = what the aircraft cost; crew plus ground staff in `men`.
// - Later generations cost more per element: the gain in fire comes from that equipment, through
//   fire_per_unit (land) or value × role share (sea, air, strategic).
// Technologies: chosen from data/research/technologies.ron; where the tree lacks the right one,
// the nearest stands in and docs/war.pt-BR.md, "Pedidos", asks for it.
(
    types: [
        // ================================== Land (element: 1,000 men) ==================================
